- a face x headgear rule table that decides what is built and discloses every adjustment in the existing 'compatibility adjustments' panel.
Planet badger/bear-like animals stop growing forehead horns.

eyeTypes.geometryAndShading:
Built by the new pure module src/fauna-composer-eye.ts as a head-local patch with 20 segments. Rings, from the centre out (radius in er):
- centre;
- R1 pupil edge .34;
- R2 duplicate at 1.06 x R1 in iris-inner colour, for a crisp edge;
- R3 iris .65;
- R4 limbus .92, iris x .45;
- R5 lid margin: an almond opening, half-height .72 above and .62 below, coloured coat x .35;
- R6 outer lid at 1.2, raised .08 er;
- R7 orbit blend at 1.55, coloured with the coat at that point (mesh glue uses coatAt).
Inner rings are clipped to .98 of the opening. The cornea dome lift is .30 er x (1 - rho^2). Pupil colour [.008,.008,.010].

Gloss: cornea (R1-R4) 1.0, lid margin .4, others 0.

Placement:
- Cast a ray from the head-local anchor (the old ex, ey at z = 0) along the gaze (yaw from the midline per face, pitched up 5 degrees, mirrored per side).
- Use the first hit on the already-built 'head' part triangles; recess the eye .2 er behind it.
- Gaze yaw (degrees from midline): flat-face 15, carnivore muzzle 35, biped or quadruped jaws 45, grazer muzzle 65, proboscis 70, sprawled jaws 70, beak 75.
- Eye size: er = k x cH x eyeScale x eyeRel, with k: beak .16, flat-face .13, grazer muzzle .14, other muzzle .13, jaws .13, proboscis .07.

About 141 vertices and 260 triangles per eye.

