workPackages.changes:
(1) Create tests/fauna-face-specimens.json at baseline (c80c8dd). Each entry has {name, text}, where text is the v2 recipe text encoded NOW, so 'after' runs decode the same animals through WP1's migration even though the wild and coherent generators change. Entries:
- the 12 presets;
- the 9 wild hybrids from the audit (composer-16, -8, -43, -40, -31, -1, -5, -26, -51);
- 4 custom overrides (ape+paired, bird+nasal, giraffe+trunk, rhinoceros+trunk);
- 4 coherent plausibles (pachyderm, primate, ungulate-grazer, theropod, using the audit's seeds);
- 6 eye showcases: giraffe, cat-like small carnivore (wild muzzle + paw, bodyLength < .9), crocodile-like sprawled jaws, bird, ape, centipede.

(2) tests/fauna-face-closeups.html plus the .browser.mjs driver, productising face-audit.mjs:
- the page builds each specimen with createComposerCreature and frames it from the creature's head bounds. headBounds() exists only after WP1, so fall back to computing it from buildComposerSkin(graph).parts when the method is missing;
- per specimen, one sheet of 6 panels: front, side, 3/4, top, eye macro (camera at the left eye along its outward normal, 4 x er distance) and whole animal; plus a JSON report of part triangle counts, eye radius and constraint list;
- CLI flags: --label <before|after|wpN>, --out (default test-results/fauna-face-eye-round-2026-10-02/<label>/), --only <name,...>, --port (default 5231, strictPort);
- start its own Vite, run the whole capture inside withGpuLock({owner:'fauna-face-closeups-<label>'}) in batches, with each hold under 30 s, release on failure, and stop Vite at the end;
- write a contact sheet <label>/contact.png (an ImageMagick montage is acceptable through the image-ops tooling, or a canvas-composited PNG from the page).

(3) Run it once at baseline: --label before.

(4) tests/tools/composer-part-diff.mjs --base <dir> --head <dir> --texts <file|builtin:skin2|builtin:specimens|builtin:graph435>:
- imports src from two source roots, e.g. a `git worktree add` of the base ref in a sibling folder, created and removed by the caller;
- for each recipe text, builds the graph and skin in both and reports per part (part + label) the triangle count and the position/colour/weight hash, rebased to part-local vertex ranges, plus per-bone rest hash;
- prints a JSON summary {changedParts: {part: count}, changedBones, recipesChanged, unchangedRecipes}.
WP4, WP5 and the coordinator use it to prove that only the predicted parts drift.

